# The Missing Link in `images[]` Arrays

In your PHP snippet, you are attempting to send multiple values for an array field named `images`. You expect the receiving API to get two distinct image strings, yet it only receives one. This behavior stems from a fundamental mismatch between how PHP constructs your string and what the cURL library actually sends to the server.

When you iterate through `$fields` with `foreach`, concatenating `$key` and `$value` into `$fields_string`, you are manually building a query-string format (e.g., `images[]=aW1hZ2Ux&amp;images[]=aW1hZ2Uy`). While this works perfectly fine for standard GET requests or when the server expects raw form data, cURL's default handling of `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS` changes behavior based on how that string is formatted.

If you pass a simple query-string to `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS`, cURL treats it as raw POST data. However, if your target API expects `multipart/form-data` (which is the standard for file uploads and base64 image payloads), passing a URL-encoded string directly often results in the server parsing only the last occurrence of the array key or failing to recognize the second value as part of the same array dimension.

The specific issue in your code is that you are manually stringifying the data before passing it to cURL. While PHP allows duplicate keys like `'images[]' => 'val1', 'images[]' => 'val2'`, simply concatenating them with `&amp;` inside a loop creates a flat query string. When cURL sends this, the receiving server might interpret the structure differently than intended, or worse, if you switch contexts to multipart uploads later, the manual string concatenation breaks entirely.

To reliably send arrays in cURL POST requests, especially when dealing with image data, you should bypass manual string construction and let PHP handle the encoding for you. The most robust approach is to pass an associative array directly to `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS`. When you do this, PHP automatically detects that the key ends with `[]` or is a numerically indexed array and generates the correct multipart form-data boundary headers.

Here is how you should refactor your code:

```php
$ch = curl_init();

// Set the URL
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, 'http://api.example.com/api');

// Enable POST mode
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POST, true);

// Prepare the data as an associative array
// Do not manually concatenate strings here. 
// PHP will handle the encoding and boundaries automatically.
$post_data = [
    'username' => 'annonymous',
    'api_key' => urlencode("1234"), // Keep manual encode if needed, but often handled by curl too
    'images[]' => base64_encode('image1'),
    'images[]' => base64_encode('image2')
];

// Pass the array directly to CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, $post_data);

// Execute
$result = curl_exec($ch);
echo $result;

curl_close($ch);
```

By passing `$post_data` (the array) instead of `$fields_string` (the concatenated string), cURL switches to sending `multipart/form-data`. This is the format almost all modern APIs require for uploading multiple values. The server will now correctly parse the payload into an array containing both image strings, rather than collapsing them or ignoring the second entry.

If you are working within a larger application stack and want to ensure consistent behavior across different PHP versions and cURL configurations, consider using `CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER` explicitly if the API requires specific content types. However, in 99% of cases, letting cURL infer the headers from the array structure is the safest route.

Avoid manually building query strings unless you are strictly sending `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` data where the server does not expect multipart boundaries. For any scenario involving images or multiple values, stick to passing raw arrays to `CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS`. This eliminates ambiguity and ensures the receiving API gets exactly what it expects: a populated array of images.

If your project is built with Laravel, this same principle applies to their HTTP facade; always pass an array to the second argument of `post()` rather than a concatenated string. For more on handling complex form data in modern PHP stacks, you might find useful patterns at https://laravelcompany.com.
